  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 0,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.2708333333333333,
            0.18567251461988304,
            0.7245370370370371,
            0.22076023391812866
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "CHARACTERIZATIONS OF BOUNDED MEAN OSCILLATION"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.3680555555555556,
            0.2309941520467836,
            0.6319444444444444,
            0.24561403508771928
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "BY CHARLES FEFFERMAN"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.2638888888888889,
            0.2543859649122807,
            0.7361111111111112,
            0.2719298245614035
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Communicated by M. H. Protter, December 14, 1970"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.13194444444444445,
            0.2777777777777778,
            0.8657407407407407,
            0.3157894736842105
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "BMO (bounded mean oscillation) is the Banach space of all functions "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "f \\in L_{\\mathrm{loc}}^{1}(R^{n})"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for which"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.22916666666666666,
            0.3230994152046784,
            0.7615740740740741,
            0.36403508771929827
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_0_equation_4.jpg",
          "content": "\\left| \\left| f \\right| \\right| _ {\\mathrm{BMO}} = \\sup _ {Q} \\left(\\frac {1}{| Q |} \\int_ {Q} | f (x) - \\operatorname{av} _ {Q} f | d x\\right) <   \\infty ,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.12731481481481483,
            0.37280701754385964,
            0.8657407407407407,
            0.4269005847953216
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "where the sup ranges over all cubes  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "Q \\subseteq R^{n}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "av_{Q} f"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  is the mean of f over Q. See [5]. For convenience, we identify f and  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "f'"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  in BMO if  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "f - f'"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  is constant."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.13194444444444445,
            0.4327485380116959,
            0.8657407407407407,
            0.4868421052631579
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "THEOREM 1. BMO is the dual of the Hardy space "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "H^{1}(R^{n})"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The inner product is given by "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\langle f, g \\rangle = \\int_{R^{n}} f(x) g(x) dx"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "f \\in BMO"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "g"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " belonging to the dense subspace of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "C^{\\infty}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " rapidly decreasing functions in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "H^{1}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.13194444444444445,
            0.4956140350877193,
            0.8657407407407407,
            0.5321637426900585
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Here, we regard "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "H^1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " as the space of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "f \\in L^1(R^n)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " whose Riesz transforms "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "R_j(f)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are all in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "L^1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". See [7]."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.13194444444444445,
            0.5380116959064327,
            0.8657407407407407,
            0.5760233918128655
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "THEOREM 2. A function belongs to BMO if and only if it can be written in the form "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "g_0 + \\sum_{j=1}^{n} R_j(g_j)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " with "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "g_0, g_1, \\cdots, g_n \\in L^\\infty(R^n)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.1574074074074074,
            0.5833333333333334,
            0.4837962962962963,
            0.6008771929824561
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note that the usual definition"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.24768518518518517,
            0.6111111111111112,
            0.7476851851851852,
            0.6505847953216374
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_0_equation_10.jpg",
          "content": "R _ {j} (g) (x) = \\lim _ {\\epsilon \\rightarrow 0; M \\rightarrow \\infty} \\int_ {\\epsilon <   | x - y | <   M} K _ {j} (x - y) f (y) d y"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.13194444444444445,
            0.6578947368421053,
            0.8657407407407407,
            0.695906432748538
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "with "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "K_{j}(y) = cy_{j} / |y|^{n + 1}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " need not make sense for all "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "g \\in L^{\\infty}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". (Consider "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "g(x) = \\operatorname{sgn}(x)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " on the line.) Therefore, we define"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.21296296296296297,
            0.7046783625730995,
            0.7685185185185185,
            0.7441520467836257
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_0_equation_12.jpg",
          "content": "R _ {j} (g) (x) = \\lim _ {\\epsilon \\rightarrow 0} \\int_ {\\epsilon <   | x - y |} \\left[ K _ {j} (x - y) - K _ {j} ^ {0} (- y) \\right] g (y) d y,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.13194444444444445,
            0.7514619883040936,
            0.8657407407407407,
            0.8055555555555556
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "where "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "K_{j}^{0}(y) = K_{j}(y)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "|y| > 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "K_{j}^{0}(y) = 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "|y| \\leq 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". This makes sense for all "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "g \\in L^{\\infty}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", and agrees with the usual definition up to an additive constant if "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "g"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " has compact support. See [3, p. 105]."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.13194444444444445,
            0.8055555555555556,
            0.8657407407407407,
            0.8421052631578947
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The main idea in proving Theorems 1 and 2 is to study the Poisson integral of a function in BMO. Recall that any function "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "f"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " satisfying"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.13194444444444445,
            0.12134502923976608,
            0.47453703703703703,
            0.15204678362573099
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "BULLETIN OF THE\nAMERICAN MATHEMATICAL SOCIETY\nVolume 77, Number 4, July 1971"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_footnote",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.1597222222222222,
            0.847953216374269,
            0.6180555555555556,
            0.8640350877192983
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "AMS 1969 subject classifications. Primary 3067, 4635."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_footnote",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.13194444444444445,
            0.8625730994152047,
            0.8657407407407407,
            0.8947368421052632
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Key words and phrases. Bounded mean oscillation, Riesz transforms, maximal function, Poisson integral."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_footnote",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.49074074074074076,
            0.8976608187134503,
            0.8657407407407407,
            0.9137426900584795
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Copyright © 1971, American Mathematical Society"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.4837962962962963,
            0.9195906432748538,
            0.5208333333333334,
            0.935672514619883
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "587"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.3587962962962963,
            0.15350877192982457,
            0.6388888888888888,
            0.19736842105263158
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_1_equation_0.jpg",
          "content": "\\int_ {R ^ {n}} \\frac {| f (x) |}{(| x | + 1) ^ {n + 1}} d x <   \\infty\\tag{*}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.12731481481481483,
            0.20760233918128654,
            0.8564814814814815,
            0.22660818713450293
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "has a Poisson integral "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "u(x, t) = \\mathrm{P.I.}(f)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " defined on "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "R_{+}^{n + 1} = R^n \\times (0, \\infty)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.12962962962962962,
            0.23391812865497075,
            0.8634259259259259,
            0.2719298245614035
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "THEOREM 3. A function "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "f"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " belongs to BMO if and only if (*) holds and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\iint_{|x - x_0| < \\delta; 0 < t < \\delta} t|\\nabla u(x, t)|^2 dx dt \\leq C\\delta^n"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for all "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x_0 \\in R^n"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\delta > 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.12962962962962962,
            0.2777777777777778,
            0.8634259259259259,
            0.3157894736842105
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Theorems 1–3 and their proofs can be used to study  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "H^{1}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . For example,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.12962962962962962,
            0.3216374269005848,
            0.8634259259259259,
            0.39473684210526316
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "THEOREM 4. Let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "F = (u_0(x, t); u_1(x, t), \\dots, u_n(x, t))"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " be an "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(n+1)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "-tuple of harmonic functions on "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "R_+^{n+1}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", satisfying the Cauchy-Riemann equations of [7]. If the nontangential maximal function "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "u_0^*(x) \\equiv \\sup_{|x'| < t; t > 0} |u_0(x - x', t)|"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " belongs to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "L^1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "F"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "H^1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.12962962962962962,
            0.40058479532163743,
            0.8657407407407407,
            0.4546783625730994
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Different techniques enable us to replace  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "L^{1}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  and  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "H^{1}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  by  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "L^{p}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  and  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "H^{p}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ",  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "0 < p < \\infty"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". This generalizes a one-dimensional result of D. Burkholder, R. Gundy, and M. Silverstein (see [1] and [2])."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.12731481481481483,
            0.45321637426900585,
            0.8657407407407407,
            0.489766081871345
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Further applications of Theorems 1–3 appear in [4] and [6]. [4] contains detailed proofs of the results stated here."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.42824074074074076,
            0.5043859649122807,
            0.5717592592592593,
            0.52046783625731
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "REFERENCES"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "ref_text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.12962962962962962,
            0.5263157894736842,
            0.8634259259259259,
            0.5555555555555556
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. D. Burkholder and R. Gundy, Extrapolation and interpolation of quasi-linear operators on martingales, Acta Math. 124 (1970), 249–304."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "ref_text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.12962962962962962,
            0.5555555555555556,
            0.8634259259259259,
            0.5847953216374269
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. D. Burkholder, R. Gundy and M. Silverstein, A maximal function characterization of the class  "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "H^{p}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "  (to appear)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "ref_text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.13194444444444445,
            0.5847953216374269,
            0.8634259259259259,
            0.6140350877192983
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. A. P. Calderón and A. Zygmund, On the existence of certain singular integrals, Acta Math. 88 (1952), 85–139. MR 14, 637."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "ref_text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.1597222222222222,
            0.6140350877192983,
            0.5324074074074074,
            0.6301169590643275
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. C. Fefferman and E. M. Stein, (in prep.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "ref_text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.13194444444444445,
            0.6286549707602339,
            0.8634259259259259,
            0.6578947368421053
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. F. John and L. Nirenberg, On functions of bounded mean oscillation, Comm. Pure Appl. Math. 14 (1961), 785–799."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "ref_text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.13194444444444445,
            0.6578947368421053,
            0.8634259259259259,
            0.6871345029239766
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. E. M. Stein, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "L^p"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " boundedness of certain convolution operators, Bull. Amer. Math. Soc. 77 (1971), 404-405."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
